---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";

const site = "https://cognitsystem.com";
const slug = "catch-weight-pricing-for-meat-and-seafood-distributors";
const url = `${site}/blog/${slug}/`;

const title = "Catch-Weight Pricing & Invoicing for NZ Meat & Seafood Distributors | Cognit";
const description =
  "A carton of lamb racks or a bin of fillets never weighs exactly what the label says. Here's why catch-weight products break ordinary order and invoicing systems — and how catch-weight pricing should actually work.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What is catch-weight, and why does it break normal ordering software?",
    a: "Catch-weight is when the actual weight of a unit — a carton, a bin, a side of meat, a tray of fillets — varies from item to item, even though it's ordered and often priced by a fixed unit like a carton or a case. Most ordering and invoicing software assumes one unit always weighs the same, so it can only invoice what was ordered, not what was actually picked, packed and shipped. That forces distributors into manual weight sheets and invoice corrections after the fact.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does Cognit have catch-weight pricing built in already?",
    a: "Catch-weight is exactly the kind of requirement we build into a distributor's system rather than sell as a generic checkbox feature — because how it needs to work differs by product, supplier and customer agreement. Tell us how your catch-weight products are ordered, weighed and invoiced today, and we'll build that logic into your Cognit system so ordering, stock and invoicing all use the real weight.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can customers still order by carton or case, even if they're invoiced on actual weight?",
    a: "Yes — that's the point. A customer can order in the units that make sense to them (cartons, cases, bins), while your system captures the actual weight at pick or pack and invoices on that real figure, with the ordered quantity and the invoiced weight both visible and reconciled, not fought over after delivery.",
  },
  {
    q: "How does catch-weight affect stock and margin reporting?",
    a: "If stock is only tracked by carton or case count, your on-hand figures and your margin are both slightly wrong, and the gap grows with volume. Tracking actual weight through stock, sales and invoicing means on-hand quantities and margin reporting reflect what you really moved and were really paid for, not a nominal average.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is this relevant outside meat and seafood?",
    a: "Any product sold in variable-weight units has the same problem — cheese, produce, some bakery and butchery lines. If your business invoices customers on a weight that's rarely the same twice, catch-weight handling is worth building into your system regardless of which food category you're in.",
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  headline: "Catch-weight pricing and invoicing for meat and seafood distributors",
  description,
  author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Cognit System", url: site },
  publisher: {
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Cognit System",
    logo: { "@type": "ImageObject", url: `${site}/logo.png` },
  },
  datePublished: "2026-08-01",
  dateModified: "2026-08-01",
  mainEntityOfPage: { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": url },
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
  })),
};
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path={`/blog/${slug}`} schema={[articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
  <Header />

  <main class="bg-cog-stone text-cog-slate">
    <!-- HERO (photographic) -->
    <section class="orbs relative isolate overflow-hidden">
      <img src="/images/food-distribution.jpg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 h-full w-full object-cover" />
      <div class="absolute inset-0 -z-10 bg-cog-ink/82"></div>
      <div class="dotgrid absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-40"></div>
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-20 pb-20 text-center sm:px-8 sm:pt-28 hero-rise">
        <span class="chip mx-auto border border-white/15 text-slate-200">Insights</span>
        <h1 class="mt-5 text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl">
          Catch-weight pricing for meat and seafood distributors
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-300">
          A carton of lamb racks never weighs exactly what the label says.
          Here's why that breaks ordinary ordering software — and what
          catch-weight pricing should actually look like.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BODY -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-16 sm:px-8">
      <article class="article">
        <p>
          Order a carton of lamb racks, a bin of snapper fillets or a case of
          cheese wheels and the label might say one thing, but the scale at
          the other end says another. That's catch-weight — products sold in
          a nominal unit like a carton or a case, where the real weight
          varies every single time. It's completely normal in meat, seafood
          and some produce and dairy lines. The problem is that most ordering
          and invoicing software was never built to handle it, so distributors
          end up bolting a manual weight sheet onto a system that assumes
          every carton weighs the same.
        </p>

        <h2>Where catch-weight breaks a normal order-to-invoice flow</h2>
        <p>
          A standard order system multiplies quantity by unit price and calls
          it done — ten cartons at a fixed carton price, invoice generated.
          Catch-weight products don't work that way: the customer might order
          ten cartons, but what actually gets picked, packed and shipped is
          ten cartons at ten slightly different weights, and the agreed price
          is per kilogram, not per carton. If the system can't capture the
          real weight against the order, someone ends up re-keying invoices
          from a handwritten weight sheet, or — worse — invoicing the nominal
          weight and quietly under- or over-charging on every single line.
        </p>
        <p>
          That gap shows up in three places at once: the customer's invoice
          doesn't match what they actually received, stock on hand drifts
          away from what's really in the chiller, and margin reporting is
          built on a weight that was never real to begin with.
        </p>

        <h2>What catch-weight pricing should actually look like</h2>
        <p>
          Fixing this isn't about a generic "catch-weight" toggle — it's
          about the actual weight flowing through the same system as the
          order, from pick to invoice:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Customers order in whatever unit makes sense to them — carton, case, bin — without needing to know the exact weight up front</li>
          <li>The real weight is captured at pick or pack, against that specific order line, not estimated afterwards</li>
          <li>Invoicing uses the actual captured weight and the agreed <a href="/features/sales">price</a>, so the invoice reflects what was really shipped</li>
          <li><a href="/features/inventory">Stock on hand</a> is adjusted by real weight moved, not a nominal carton count</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Done this way, "what do we invoice this customer" stops depending
          on a weight sheet someone has to chase down after the truck's
          already left, and margin reporting reflects what actually moved
          rather than a rounded-off average.
        </p>

        <h2>Why this compounds at volume</h2>
        <p>
          A handful of catch-weight cartons a week is a manageable manual
          correction. Hundreds of cartons a day, across dozens of customers
          each with their own <a href="/blog/customer-specific-pricing-for-wholesalers">agreed pricing</a>,
          is a different problem entirely — and it's exactly where small,
          unnoticed gaps between nominal and actual weight add up to a real
          margin leak, invoice disputes, and
          <a href="/blog/inventory-stock-control-software-for-wholesalers">stock counts</a>
          nobody quite trusts. It's the same pattern we see across
          <a href="/blog/erp-for-food-and-beverage-distributors">food and beverage distribution</a>
          generally — the pressure points are specific to the category, but
          the fix is the same: real data flowing through one system instead
          of being reconstructed by hand afterwards.
        </p>

        <h2>Built into your system, not a bolt-on module</h2>
        <p>
          Catch-weight handling isn't something we sell as an off-the-shelf
          feature, because how it needs to work varies by product, supplier
          agreement and customer — some businesses invoice strictly on actual
          weight, others round to agreed bands, others handle it differently
          again by product line. Tell us how your catch-weight products are
          ordered, weighed and invoiced today, and we build that logic into
          your Cognit system so it works with your real
          <a href="/features/sales">pricing</a>,
          <a href="/features/inventory">stock</a> and
          <a href="/features/invoicing">invoicing</a> — the same approach we
          take for <a href="/industries/food-distribution">food distribution</a>
          more broadly: tell us your problem, we build it into your system.
        </p>

        <h2>Getting catch-weight off the weight sheet</h2>
        <p>
          If your invoices for meat, seafood or other variable-weight lines
          still depend on a handwritten weight sheet making it back to the
          office, it's worth talking through how that should actually flow
          through your system. See <a href="/pricing">pricing</a> or
          <a href="/contact">get in touch</a> to talk through your catch-weight
          products specifically.
        </p>

        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <>
            <h3>{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </>
        ))}
      </article>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
